---
// Contributing: every way to help, from a two-minute bug report to building the renderer. Same content as the repo's
// CONTRIBUTING.md (keep the two in step). Real commands only (renderer/README.md, .github/workflows/deploy.yml).
import Docs, { type Heading } from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/nav';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/mrdarrengriffin/facet';
const issue = (t: string) => `${REPO}/issues/new?template=${t}.yml`;
const headings: Heading[] = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'bug', text: 'Report a bug' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'device', text: 'Test on a device' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'docs', text: 'Improve the docs' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'code', text: 'Contribute code' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'site', text: 'The docs site' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'building', text: 'Building & testing' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'adapter', text: 'Adding a data source' },
  { depth: 3, slug: 'pull-requests', text: 'Pull requests' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'ideas', text: 'Share an idea' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'kind', text: 'Be kind' },
];
---

<Docs edit="site/src/pages/contributing.astro" title="Contributing" description="Every way to help Facet, and most of them need no code." group="Help" current={url('/contributing/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Thanks for helping. Facet has one maintainer and two supported panels so far, so a test report or a docs fix makes a
    real difference, and most of the ways in need no code.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>I want to...</th><th>Start here</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tell you something is broken</td><td><a href="#bug">Report a bug</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Try Facet on my panel</td><td><a href="#device">Test on a device</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fix a typo or explain something better</td><td><a href="#docs">Improve the docs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Write code</td><td><a href="#code">Contribute code</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Suggest a screen, a component or a better way</td><td><a href="#ideas">Share an idea</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <p>Not sure where something fits? <a href={`${REPO}/issues/new/choose`}>Open an issue</a> and ask.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bug">Report a bug</h2>
  <p><a href={issue('bug')}>Open a bug report</a>. The most useful reports say:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>what you did, what you expected, and what happened instead;</li>
    <li>where: which panel, or the docs site and which browser;</li>
    <li>the firmware version, if it happened on a panel (Home Assistant shows it on the panel's device page);</li>
    <li>a photo or screenshot, if it is something you can see;</li>
    <li>the panel's problem report: <a href={url('/help/troubleshooting/#report')}>Report a problem</a> has the steps.
      It keeps the logs from before a restart, Wi-Fi and Home Assistant drops and the last crash, with tokens, keys and passwords removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="device">Test on a device</h2>
  <p>Real panels find what the browser can't: timing, touch, heat and Wi-Fi drop-outs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Panels:</strong> the Waveshare ESP32-P4-86 (4 inch, 720 x 720) and the Guition ESP32-S3-4848S040 (4 inch,
      480 x 480). Other panels come after 1.0: if you have one, an issue saying so helps decide which.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Install Facet</strong> from your browser on the <a href={url('/install/')}>Install</a> page. It
      replaces what is on the panel now; updates then arrive in Home Assistant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What to try:</strong> go round every screen, tap, swipe and long press, switch dark and light, and leave it
      running for a day. Look out for clipped text, misaligned edges, wrong colours, slow or janky animation, controls that
      don't do what they say, and freezes or reboots.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Then send a <a href={issue('device-test')}>device test report</a>: panel model, firmware version, what worked, what
    broke. "Everything worked" is a useful report too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="docs">Improve the docs</h2>
  <p>
    Every docs page has an <strong>Edit this page on GitHub</strong> link at the bottom. It opens the file that holds that
    page's text: edit it in the browser and GitHub turns it into a pull request for you. Spotted something but don't want
    to edit? Use <strong>Report a problem</strong> next to it, or <a href={issue('docs')}>open a docs fix</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some pages are generated: a component's description comes from the comment at the top of its <code>.cpp</code> file,
    a screen's from its spec in <code>renderer/specs/</code>. The edit link takes you to the right file either way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="code">Contribute code</h2>
  <p>
    The repository has six parts: <code>renderer/</code> (the C++/LVGL renderer: core, foundations, components,
    screens, specs, tokens and the tools that check them), <code>firmware/</code> (the ESPHome external component),
    <code>esphome/</code> (one YAML per board and the packages they share), <code>workbench/</code> (the local web app
    for working on Facet), <code>boards/</code> (one file per supported panel) and <code>site/</code> (these docs,
    generated from what the renderer registers).
    The <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/renderer/README.md`}>renderer README</a> has the detail.
  </p>

  <h3 id="site">The docs site</h3>
  <p>The site is built around the docs flavour of the WASM renderer, so it needs the setup under Building &amp; testing below. A text-only fix needs nothing: use the page's <strong>Edit this page on GitHub</strong> link.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="Shell" code={`FW_FEATURES=docs renderer/build.sh   # the docs flavour of the WASM build
cd site && npm ci
npm run dev                          # or: npm run build`} />

  <h3 id="building">Building &amp; testing</h3>
  <p>
    Everything goes through one command at the repo root, <code>./facet</code> (<code>./facet help</code> lists it). It
    needs Node 24, Python 3 and Emscripten 6.0.13 (LVGL, ArduinoJson, <code>lv_conf.h</code> and the fonts are in the
    repository); a firmware build also needs ESPHome. The first run does <code>npm ci</code> for you.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>When</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>./facet build wasm [1x|1.5x]</code></td><td>The renderer as WebAssembly (1.5x = the 720 panel, 1x = the 480 panel; both by default)</td><td>While you work</td><td>~35 s a density</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>./facet build firmware &lt;board&gt;</code></td><td>A panel's firmware (<code>waveshare-p4-86</code>, <code>guition-4848s040</code>): the factory and OTA images</td><td>To try a change on a panel</td><td>~3 min; seconds when nothing changed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>./facet check quick [--ids a,b]</code></td><td>Build 1.5x, then the quick checks (tokens, contrast, contract, host tests, the rules) and the functional tests of what you name</td><td>Before every commit</td><td>10 s to 2 min</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>./facet check full</code></td><td>Both builds, then every check at both densities: every rule, every functional test, the image decoders, the editor's browser tests</td><td>CI on every push and pull request; before a release</td><td>~10 min</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>./facet check nightly</code></td><td>Full, then the slow ones: memory errors (ASan), theme fuzzing, frame times, the bench</td><td>Overnight</td><td>~30 min</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Each step prints one PASS / FAIL line with its time; a failure shows its error lines and the log file. Add
    <code>--no-build</code> to check the build that is already there; <code>node renderer/watch.mjs</code> rebuilds on
    save while you work. The house rules, in short: every value comes from a token, sizes sit on the 4 px grid,
    everything works at 720 and 480 in dark and light, and screens are made of real components. The checks hold them.
    If the build setup gets in your way, open an issue: getting a second person building Facet is worth fixing.
  </p>

  <h3 id="adapter">Adding a data source (adapter)</h3>
  <p>
    A screen's data can come from a public API or a Home Assistant integration. Each source is an adapter: a row in
    <code>renderer/sources/sources.json</code>, the source in the screen's spec, a branch in the feed's
    <code>renderer/core/&lt;feed&gt;_source.cpp</code> that maps the source onto the screen's model, and a test on the
    fake Home Assistant or fake web. No screen changes. <a href={url('/inside/adapters/')}>Writing an adapter</a> has
    the steps, a worked example, each screen's model and the checklist.
  </p>

  <h3 id="pull-requests">Pull requests</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Fork, then branch from <code>main</code>.</li>
    <li>Keep each pull request to one thing: one fix, one component, one page.</li>
    <li>Say what changed and why; for anything visible, add a screenshot at 720 and 480.</li>
    <li>Commit messages: a short summary line, <code>Area: what changed</code> (for example <code>Docs: contributing page</code>), then detail below if it helps.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Small pull requests get reviewed fastest. For anything big, open an issue first so you can agree the shape before you spend the time.</p>

  <h2 id="ideas">Share an idea</h2>
  <p>
    New screens, components, panels, sounds, better words: start a thread in <a href="https://github.com/mrdarrengriffin/facet/discussions/categories/ideas">Discussions &gt; Ideas</a>. <a href={url('/updates/#next')}>What's next</a>
    on the Updates page lists what is being built now.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kind">Be kind</h2>
  <p>
    Everyone taking part agrees to the <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`}>Code of Conduct</a>. By
    contributing you agree your work is released under the project's MIT licence.
  </p>
</Docs>
